Next.js provides dedicated server-only functions within the App Router (`next/headers`), such as `cookies()` and `headers()`, which allow Server Components to read incoming request metadata directly within component code:
```tsx
import { cookies, headers } from 'next/headers';
import { db } from '@/lib/db';
import { decryptSession } from '@/lib/session';
import { redirect } from 'next/navigation';

export default async function DashboardPage() {
  const cookieStore = await cookies(); // In Next.js 15, cookies() is asynchronous
  const sessionToken = cookieStore.get('session_token')?.value;

  if (!sessionToken) {
    redirect('/login');
  }

  const session = await decryptSession(sessionToken);
  if (!session?.userId) {
    redirect('/login');
  }

  // Safe direct database query using server-only credentials
  const user = await db.user.findUnique({
    where: { id: session.userId },
    select: { id: true, name: true, role: true }, // Never select password hashes
  });

  return <DashboardView user={user} />;
}
```

This architecture provides robust security guarantees:

